WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.511
A payment's status is one value that several services read and write.

2
00:00:07.511 --> 00:00:08.836
It says authorized.

3
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.368
The capture service writes captured.

4
00:00:11.368 --> 00:00:15.331
That takes 60 milliseconds, drawn here as a bar on a timeline.

5
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:20.729
Meanwhile, ledger reads from 5 to 15 milliseconds and gets authorized.

6
00:00:20.729 --> 00:00:23.995
Fraud reads from 20 to 30 and gets captured.

7
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:25.247
Is that allowed?

8
00:00:25.247 --> 00:00:32.137
Linearizability says yes, if you can pick one instant inside each bar where the operation took effect.

9
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:35.402
Ledger at 10, the write at 18, fraud at 25.

10
00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:40.325
Replayed in that order on one copy, each read gets the answer it got.

11
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.389
Then email reads from 40 to 50, and gets authorized.

12
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:49.202
Its instant must be after 40, when fraud has already seen captured.

13
00:00:49.202 --> 00:00:51.138
One copy would say captured.

14
00:00:51.138 --> 00:00:52.245
No choice works.

15
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:54.496
That history isn't linearizable.

16
00:00:54.496 --> 00:01:00.297
Once any read has returned the new value, every read that starts later must return it too.

17
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.411
Now a store with three copies, each holding 8.

18
00:01:03.411 --> 00:01:06.828
Every write and every read waits for two of the three.

19
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.511
A writer sends 9.

20
00:01:08.511 --> 00:01:14.248
It reaches R1 at 10 milliseconds, but R2 and R3 only at 70 and 75.

21
00:01:14.248 --> 00:01:17.464
Until then, the write isn't finished.

22
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:21.016
Reader A asks from 20 to 40 milliseconds.

23
00:01:21.016 --> 00:01:24.069
R1 and R2 answer first, with 9 and 8.

24
00:01:24.069 --> 00:01:26.379
It returns the newer one, 9.

25
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.415
Reader B starts at 48, after A has finished.

26
00:01:30.415 --> 00:01:33.622
R2 and R3 answer first, both still 8.

27
00:01:33.622 --> 00:01:34.663
B returns 8.

28
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:38.134
B started after A finished, yet saw an older value.

29
00:01:38.134 --> 00:01:41.759
Two of three overlap only for writes that have finished.

30
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:46.207
The fix: before reader A answers, it writes 9 back to two replicas.

31
00:01:46.207 --> 00:01:50.544
Every later read then finds it, at the price of a second round trip.

32
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.577
Linearizable means one copy, one timeline.

33
00:01:53.577 --> 00:01:58.372
Once anyone has seen a value, nobody who asks later sees an older one.
